Subject: HIST: Laissez-nous quoi?
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

>From: extr@jido.b30.ingr.com (Craig Presson)

>In <9212241846.AA08084@churchy.gnu.ai.mit.edu>, D Anton Sherwood writes:
>|> > One of the men, Legendre, is
>|> > supposed to have replied, "Laissez-nous faire!", meaning, "leave us
>|> > alone".
>|>  
>|> A closer translation imho:  "Let us get on with it."

>Well, maybe. "Faire" is the verb for "to do" and "to make". So,
>a closer English equivalent would be "Let us do it {ourselves}",
>with overtones of "build".

Pardon, but "Laissez" is a second person conjugation, and it
definately implies "(You) (should) permit"; the phrase is really quite
easily translated, if you want to be very literal, as "permit us to
do", with the traditional "leave us alone" translation being quite
reasonable. However, I can't see any overtones of "build" or "lets do
it ourselves"; those aren't even remotely correct. The implication is
very strongly one of "you must permit us", or even "you must not
interfere with our doing", but not "we should do".

Perry

