>From: D Anton Sherwood <dasher@well.sf.ca.us>

>Oh?  Merchants issue coupons and claim-checks all the time.

>What's stopping rival money, I'm told, is that any transaction using it is
>legally a sale and thus taxable -- that is, if I buy a bottle of Windex with
>CrowleyCash, redeemable in hubcaps, I pay sales tax on the Windex, and
>Safeway pays sales tax on the hubcaps.

There are states without sales taxes. In spite of this, there is no
competing currency. Thats because its explictly illegal for anyone but
the fed to issue banknotes. Don't try applying reasoning like "but
people issue coupons", because thats not how governments think. Any
currency other than greenbacks is not for use inside the US. There ARE
certain things that are possible, like travelers checks, and it would,
concievably, be possible to try to start alternative currencies
through various subtrefuges, but if you are talking about bank notes
qua bank notes, forget it.

Perry

