An excellent point was raised about whether I was correct in claiming
that printing competing money (paper bank notes, specifically, altho
I was not nearly that specific) was legal in the U.S.  I found I
was unable to back my claim, altho Perry agree that it was, in fact,
illegal.

In view of my own inability to prove the point conclusively (limited
references in the house, you know), I'm going to back off by pointing
out that (a) banks used to do this and (b) they were forced to
stop after the Banking Acts of 1863, 1864 and 1865, which effectively
taxed state bank notes out of existence.  I think it makes the same
point equally well.

I also turned up an amusing definition of currency.  Apparently the
term originated in the American colonies and included "tobacco
in Virginia and sugar in the West Indies", as well as the more usual
paper and metal monies, nicely backing the point about valuta.

I added both of the above to the essay, and will, at some point in the
near future, post to exi-essay.

Of course, I'm still looking for more suggestions/critique.  Thanks!

